```

If you can write those six from memory, you pass the wrap question.

## `wrap` and logging

Wrapped objects appear in the Command Log. Do not wrap huge fixture arrays if you only need `.its('0.email')` — log noise. Prefer `cy.fixture().its('0.email')`.

## `null` vs wrap

`cy.wrap(null)` is valid (subject null). Tasks return `null` in Node; in the browser you wrap values you want to assert. Do not `cy.wrap(undefined)` as a substitute for skipping — skip with `this.skip()` instead.

## When wrap + should retries a function (use 6, variant)

```ts
cy.wrap({}).should(() => {
  expect(window.localStorage.getItem('flash')).to.eq('saved');
});
```

Retries until localStorage matches. Prefer asserting a toast in the DOM. This pattern is for values Cypress does not query by default.

## Interview drill

"List six uses of `cy.wrap`." Primitive, object, Promise, jQuery/DOM, custom-command yield, re-enter the queue after `.then`. "Does `cy.wrap('#id')` find an element?" No.

## Part 2 close

You can structure suites, query the DOM, explain the queue, wait via assertions, name aliases, pick should vs expect, and put values back on the chain with wrap. Stop using `await cy` and `cy.wait(ms)` as your default tools.
## Wrap vs get vs contains (selector confusion)

```ts
cy.wrap('Submit');                 // subject is the string "Submit"
cy.contains('Submit');             // subject is a DOM node
cy.wrap(Cypress.$('button'));      // jQuery now, no initial retry to find it
cy.get('button');                  // retries until found
```

`Cypress.$` + wrap is a smell for finding elements. Use `get`.

## Custom command anti-pattern

```ts
Cypress.Commands.add('badToday', () => {
  return new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10); // not on the queue
});
```

```ts
Cypress.Commands.add('goodToday', () => {
  cy.wrap(new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10));
});
```

Use 5.

## Wrapping `undefined`

`cy.wrap(undefined)` yields undefined; following `.should('eq', 1)` fails. Tasks cannot return undefined; wrap can wrap it. Still prefer not to.

## Performance

Wrapping giant DOM trees in `.each` is normal. Wrapping 10,000-row JSON and `should('deep.equal')` is slow and a bad assertion. Slice first.

